# Getting Started, part 3: what Strata is.  The terms are defined before they
# are used: the two kinds of documents first, then the outline.
.h1 About Strata [about]
Strata is a technical document processing system for writing long documents,
such as reports, proposals and manuals.  The hands stay on the keyboard, the
shape of a long document stays in order, and the pages are set through LaTeX,
so that they come out well made.
.h2 Two kinds of documents [kinds]
A document is one of two kinds.  You choose the kind when you make the
document.
.h3 Unstructured documents [kind-flat]
An unstructured document is text in {b:blocks}.  A block is what you know as a
paragraph: text that ends with the {k:Enter} key.  A block can also have several
{b:columns}; a block of several columns is a table.  Letters, memos and lists
are unstructured documents.  The blocks follow each other in a row, and that
is all that there is to say about the shape of the document.
.h3 Structured documents [kind-outline]
A structured document is for the big ideas that need an outline.  Its text is
divided into {b:sections}.  A section has a headline, which is its first block,
then more blocks of text, and then sections of its own below it.  So a
structured document is a tree of sections: an outline, like the contents of a
book.  Each section has a number that comes from its place in the tree, and the
numbers change by themselves when you add, move or take away a section.
.h3 How the outline works [outline]
You can fold a structured document up, to see only its shape, and open it again
to read and write. {k:Alt-1} shows the headlines of the first level and nothing
below them; {k:Alt-2} opens the second level too, and so on to {k:Alt-9}. The
keypad keys {k:Keypad +}, {k:Keypad -} and {k:Keypad *} open and fold sections.
Other commands add a section next to the one that you are in or below it, join
two sections, and move a section in the outline. The Tutorial shows each of
them.
.h3 Which to choose [kind-choose]
Use an unstructured document for anything that is a few pages of text.  Use a
structured document for anything long that you want to find your way around.
A document made unstructured can be turned into a structured one later, with
{b:Edit}, {b:Structured}: the whole text becomes one section.  A structured
document can be turned back only while it is still one section with no sections
below it, with {b:Edit}, {b:Unstructured}.  So choose with care when you start.
.h2 Strata is not WYSIWYG [wysiwyg]
Strata does not show the page as it will be printed while you type, and that
is a deliberate choice.  A program that has to draw every page while you type
is slower, and the time spent waiting comes out of the time to write.  The
text and its outline are shown, and the screen keeps up with your fingers.  The
window version shows equations, pictures and keycaps in the text as images, but
they are static images that give an idea of how the text will look.
The layout of the finished pages is seen in either of two ways.  {b:Preview}
shows the real pages in a window of the Viewer.  {b:Print} to a PDF file makes
the real pages as a file, which is then opened in a PDF viewer.  Both typeset
the document with LaTeX, so both show the same pages.  Most of the time Preview
is the better choice, for these reasons:
- Preview writes no file that has to be named, kept or replaced.  Print to a
  PDF file writes the file named in its Output File field.
- The pages appear in the Viewer as soon as they are typeset.  A PDF file has
  to be opened in a PDF viewer first.
- Preview can show only the block or the section that holds the cursor, so a
  single change is checked without typesetting the whole document.
- Closing the Viewer ({k:Q} or {k:Esc}) returns to the Editor at once, so the
  cycle of changing the text and looking at the page is short.

Print is the way to get the final PDF file, or the pages on paper.
.h2 Who Strata is for [who]
Strata is for people who do serious work with their hands on the keyboard:
writers, engineers and editors who produce long documents and want to stay on
the home row while they do it.  Everything that Strata does is reached by a key.
A command has a key, or a short path of menu keys, a panel is filled in by
typing, and nothing is hidden behind a button that has to be found and clicked.
The price is that you learn the keys.  The Tutorial teaches them in short
sessions, and the Key Reference puts them on one sheet of paper to pin up by
your desk until you no longer need to look.
.h2 The documentation [books]
Strata comes with all of its documentation, and the documentation is in the
file format of Strata itself.  Few document processing systems ship every manual
as a document of their own, which you can open in the program, and read as a
blueprint of how to use it.  The documents of the set are:
- {b:Getting Started}, this document, is the guide that takes you from the
  system that Strata needs, through the install, to your first document, and
  teaches Strata in short sessions with documents that you type and keep.
- {b:The Reference Manual} is a real reference manual.  It is not a help file
  and it is not a web search: it describes every menu, every marker and every
  file, and how the program really works.
- {b:The Key Reference} is a leaflet of the keys, to print and to fold in
  three.
- {b:The Math Symbols card} is a sheet of every symbol that an equation can
  have, to print on both sides and to fold in three.

All of these documents are written using what Strata can do: sections, keycaps
such as {k:F12}, pictures, tables, footnotes{fn:A footnote is a marker that
holds text. Strata prints it at the foot of the page.} and equations such as
{eq:\sum_{{k=1}}^{{n}} k = \frac{{n(n+1)}}{{2}}}. Open them in Strata to see
how they are made: each one is a working example of the features that it
describes.
.h2 How the books show keys [conventions]
A key is shown in a box, like a key of the keyboard: {k:Enter}, {k:Ctrl-S},
{k:Alt-F12}.  A name with a hyphen means that you press and hold the first key,
and press the second.  The names of menus and panels are in bold, such as
{b:Print} and {b:Save Entire Document}; the names of files, commands and
settings are in the program font, such as {c:memo.doc} and {c:config}.  A line
set apart in the program font is something to type, as it stands.
